; SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later

; =============================================================================
; DashboardDemo — four cards and a chart that is really drawn
; =============================================================================
; shadcn's dashboard-01, and the first page here that puts a component from
; ANOTHER gallery on the same surface as this one's.
;
; --- THE CHART IS A VELA RENDER, NOT A PICTURE OF ONE ------------------------
;
; The area chart is a Vega specification, run by `VlRuntime`, turned into draw
; commands by `VlSceneCommands`, and appended to this page's display list by
; `VlEvgList`. Nothing is traced, embedded or approximated: the ninety points
; below go in as data and the outline that comes out is the one Vela computed.
;
; There were two routes and the difference matters. An element can carry an SVG
; string in `svgSource` and `EVGDisplayList` will import it — that works, and it
; drops every LABEL, because the importer walks vector items and a `<text>` is
; not one. Forty-seven commands of geometry and not a single axis tick. So this
; uses the other bridge, `VlEvgList`, which was written for precisely this case
; and emits geometry and text together: seventy-two commands, twenty-five of
; them words.
;
; The consequence is that the chart is not in the ELEMENT tree. It is painted
; into the display list at the position the layout put its card, which is why
; `chartBox` is a placeholder with an id and no children: the tree owns where
; the chart goes and Vela owns what is drawn there. A reader gets the card's
; label and the summary under it, because a hundred path commands are not
; something to announce.
;
; --- WHAT A CARD IS ----------------------------------------------------------
;
; A rounded box with a header row, a big number and a footer line. It is not a
; controller: there is nothing to control. `TableCtl` exists because sorting,
; paging and selection are hard; a card is a box with four strings in it, and
; putting it behind a controller would be ceremony.
;
; --- THE DATA TABLE, AND WHO OWNS THE ROW ORDER ------------------------------
;
; Two controllers, and the reason there are two is that they answer different
; questions. `TableCtl` is measured against TanStack for sorting, paging and
; selection; `SortableCtl` is measured against dnd-kit for what a drag does.
; Neither knows about the other.
;
; So ONE of them owns the order, and it is `TableCtl.records`. `SortableCtl` is
; the GESTURE — pick up, move, drop or cancel, with dnd-kit's announcements —
; and a completed drop writes the order it arrived at back into the records.
; The alternative was two lists to keep in step, which is how a table ends up
; showing a row twice.
;
; A SORT then replaces a hand-made order, which is not a compromise: sorting by
; Target is a claim about where every row belongs, and honouring a drag inside
; it would mean the column header lied. The demo says so under the table.
;
; --- THE SIDEBAR --------------------------------------------------------------
;
; A NAVIGATION LANDMARK, which is the only reason it is worth building rather
; than drawing: a column of nine buttons is nine things a reader walks past,
; and a named landmark is one thing they jump to.
;
; The page got WIDER for it instead of the content getting narrower. That is
; not politeness towards the chart, it is arithmetic: `chartW` is 994 because
; the card's inner width is 994, and every axis-label position measured against
; that number would have had to be measured again. 1336 = 255 + 1 + 1080.
;
; Two things it says that a picture cannot. `aria-current="page"` marks the one
; link you are on — set from the same test as the fill, so the two audiences
; cannot be told different stories — and the inbox button carries the name its
; envelope glyph does not.
;
; --- AND THE PAGE SCROLLS -----------------------------------------------------
;
; `pageH` is a VIEWPORT now. The sidebar is outside the scroll container and
; stands still; everything else is inside one and moves, which is the whole
; reason a sidebar is worth having — a navigation you have to scroll back up to
; reach is a navigation that is only there some of the time.
;
; EVG could not do this until this page needed it: `overflow: hidden` clipped
; and nothing scrolled, and a flex column with a definite height SHRANK
; children that overflowed it. See `oracle/scroll_check.mjs` for the browser
; behaviour that decides all of it.
;
; The nine links lead to six builders. The four Documents links and More differ
; by WHAT THEY LIST and by nothing else, so they share one — five near-identical
; page functions would have looked different here and identical on the screen.
;
; --- THE ICONS ARE ARTWORK, NOT GLYPHS ----------------------------------------
;
; Nine lucide icons, verbatim from lucide-static 1.37.0, through the mechanism
; the timeline demo already proved. They were text glyphs and they sat low
; against their labels, for two reasons of which only one was a mistake — see
; the note above `svgOpen`. A 16x16 `path` element is a box, and a box has no
; baseline to be low against.
;
; --- TWO PALETTES, ONE LAYOUT -------------------------------------------------
;
; `setTheme("marine")` and the page is a harbour rather than a white sheet. A
; theme here is a NAME, handed to `applyTree` and nothing else: the sheet holds
; both palettes — `.theme-marine .db-card` beats `.db-card` — so not one
; element gains or loses a class and there is no second tree to keep in step.
;
; The theme declares colours and nothing else. That is the rule that makes it
; a paint-only change — the layout signatures of the two plans are identical,
; so `EVGStyleSheet` reports the pass layout-clean and no box is measured
; again — and it is also what makes leaving one work at all: an element holds
; whatever the last plan wrote to it, so a themed declaration with no unscoped
; counterpart would survive the switch home and stay there. `dashboard-check`
; asserts that pairing over the sheet's own text.
;
; The two things NOT on an element are themed the same way. The chart is
; Vela's and the scroll indicator is two rectangles, so neither can carry a
; class — but their colours are in the sheet anyway, on `.db-ink-*` classes
; nothing wears, and `ink` asks the sheet what one resolves to under the theme
; that is showing. A theme that could repaint the frame and not the picture
; inside it would not be worth having.
;
; --- WHAT IS NOT HERE YET ----------------------------------------------------
;
; The reference's horizontal scroll, which it has because its sidebar takes the
; width — this table is sized to its card instead, so there is nothing to
; scroll and no scroll container to get wrong. The sidebar does not collapse to
; icons; that is a width the page does not have. And the scrollbar is an
; indicator rather than a control — see `displayListJson`.
; =============================================================================

Import "../src/RadioGroupCtl.rgr"
Import "../src/TableCtl.rgr"
Import "../src/SortableCtl.rgr"
Import "../src/VirtualCtl.rgr"
Import "../src/UiCtl.rgr"
Import "../../evg/EVGElement.rgr"
Import "../../evg/EVGUnit.rgr"
Import "../../evg/EVGLayout.rgr"
Import "../../evg/EVGDisplayList.rgr"
Import "../../evg/EVGStyleSheet.rgr"
Import "../../evg/EVGA11yTree.rgr"
Import "../../evg/EVGA11yFromTree.rgr"
Import "../../evg/EVGHitTest.rgr"
Import "../../evg/EVGInspect.rgr"
Import "../../vela/src/VlRuntime.rgr"
Import "../../vela/src/VlCommand.rgr"
Import "../../vela/src/VlJson.rgr"
Import "../../vela/src/VlEvgList.rgr"
Import "../../game_engine/ui/UITextRenderer.rgr"

treefactory Db {
    child addChild
    text  textContent

    tag Page    EVGElement (props (className "db-page"))
    tag Main    EVGElement (props (className "db-main") (role "region") (a11yLabel "Dashboard"))
    tag Cards   EVGElement (props (className "db-cards") (role "list") (a11yLabel "Key figures"))

    tag Card    EVGElement (props (className "db-card") (role "listitem"))
    tag CardTop EVGElement (props (className "db-card-top"))
    tag CardLbl EVGElement (props (className "db-card-label") (elementType 1))
    tag Badge   EVGElement (props (className "db-badge"))
    tag BadgeUp EVGElement (props (className "db-badge db-badge-up"))
    tag BadgeTx EVGElement (props (className "db-badge-text") (elementType 1))
    tag Big     EVGElement (props (className "db-big") (elementType 1))
    tag Foot    EVGElement (props (className "db-card-foot"))
    tag FootTop EVGElement (props (className "db-card-foot-top"))
    tag Trend   EVGElement (props (className "db-trend") (elementType 1))
    ; The arrow beside the trend line. Decoration: "up" is already in the
    ; sentence beside it, and a glyph read aloud after it says it twice.
    tag Arrow   EVGElement (props (className "db-arrow") (elementType 1) (a11yHidden true))
    tag Sub     EVGElement (props (className "db-card-sub") (elementType 1))

    tag Chart   EVGElement (props (className "db-chart-card"))
    tag ChartHd EVGElement (props (className "db-chart-head"))
    tag ChartTx EVGElement (props (className "db-chart-head-text"))
    tag Title   EVGElement (props (className "db-chart-title") (elementType 1) (role "heading") (a11yLevel 2))
    tag ChartSub EVGElement (props (className "db-chart-sub") (elementType 1))
    tag Spacer  EVGElement (props (className "db-spacer"))
    tag Segs    EVGElement (props (className "db-segs") (role "radiogroup") (a11yLabel "Range"))
    tag Seg     EVGElement (props (className "db-seg") (role "radio"))
    tag SegOn   EVGElement (props (className "db-seg db-seg-on") (role "radio"))
    tag SegTx   EVGElement (props (className "db-seg-text") (elementType 1))
    ; Where the chart goes. A placeholder with an id and nothing in it: the
    ; tree owns the position and Vela owns the drawing.
    tag ChartBox EVGElement (props (className "db-chart-box") (role "img"))

    ; --- the table ------------------------------------------------------------
    tag Tabs    EVGElement (props (className "db-tabs") (role "tablist") (a11yLabel "Sections"))
    tag Tab     EVGElement (props (className "db-tab") (role "tab"))
    tag TabOn   EVGElement (props (className "db-tab db-tab-on") (role "tab"))
    tag TabTx   EVGElement (props (className "db-tab-text") (elementType 1))
    ; The count beside a tab name. Part of the tab's NAME rather than a node of
    ; its own — "Past Performance 3" is one thing a reader hears, and a pill
    ; announced separately after it is a number with nothing attached.
    tag TabPill EVGElement (props (className "db-tab-pill") (a11yHidden true))
    tag PillTx  EVGElement (props (className "db-tab-pill-text") (elementType 1))
    tag Tools   EVGElement (props (className "db-tools"))
    tag Tool    EVGElement (props (className "db-tool") (role "button"))
    tag ToolTx  EVGElement (props (className "db-tool-text") (elementType 1))
    tag ToolIc  EVGElement (props (className "db-tool-icon") (elementType 1) (a11yHidden true))

    tag Table   EVGElement (props (className "db-table") (role "table") (a11yLabel "Document sections"))
    tag Head    EVGElement (props (className "db-thead") (role "rowgroup"))
    tag Body    EVGElement (props (className "db-tbody") (role "rowgroup"))
    tag Row     EVGElement (props (className "db-tr") (role "row"))
    tag RowOn   EVGElement (props (className "db-tr db-tr-on") (role "row"))
    tag Th      EVGElement (props (className "db-th") (role "columnheader"))
    tag Td      EVGElement (props (className "db-td") (role "cell"))
    tag CellTx  EVGElement (props (className "db-cell-text") (elementType 1))
    tag Strong  EVGElement (props (className "db-cell-text db-cell-strong") (elementType 1))
    tag Chip    EVGElement (props (className "db-chip"))
    tag ChipTx  EVGElement (props (className "db-chip-text") (elementType 1))
    tag Status  EVGElement (props (className "db-status"))
    tag StatusD EVGElement (props (className "db-status db-status-done"))
    tag StatIc  EVGElement (props (className "db-status-icon") (elementType 1) (a11yHidden true))
    tag StatIcD EVGElement (props (className "db-status-icon db-status-icon-done") (elementType 1) (a11yHidden true))
    tag StatTx  EVGElement (props (className "db-status-text") (elementType 1))
    tag Grip    EVGElement (props (className "db-grip") (role "button"))
    tag GripIc  EVGElement (props (className "db-grip-icon") (elementType 1) (a11yHidden true))
    tag Check   EVGElement (props (className "db-check") (role "checkbox"))
    tag Tick    EVGElement (props (className "db-tick") (elementType 1) (a11yHidden true))
    tag More    EVGElement (props (className "db-more") (role "button"))
    tag MoreIc  EVGElement (props (className "db-more-icon") (elementType 1) (a11yHidden true))
    tag Assign  EVGElement (props (className "db-assign") (role "combobox"))
    tag AssignT EVGElement (props (className "db-assign-text") (elementType 1))
    tag Chevron EVGElement (props (className "db-chevron") (elementType 1) (a11yHidden true))
    ; The window the rows are drawn in, slid up by the part of the first row
    ; that is scrolled past. See `tableCard` for why there are no spacers.
    tag Window  EVGElement (props (className "db-window"))
    tag Live    EVGElement (props (className "db-live") (elementType 1) (role "status"))

    tag Note    EVGElement (props (className "db-note") (elementType 1))

    ; --- the sidebar ----------------------------------------------------------
    ; A NAVIGATION landmark. That is not decoration: it is the thing that lets a
    ; reader jump straight to the page's links, and a column of buttons with no
    ; landmark around them is a column a reader has to walk.
    tag Side     EVGElement (props (className "db-side") (role "navigation") (a11yLabel "Main"))
    ; The hairline between the sidebar and the page. An ELEMENT, because
    ; `border-right` is dropped in silence — see the note in `sidebar`.
    tag SideEdge EVGElement (props (className "db-side-edge") (a11yHidden true))
    tag Content  EVGElement (props (className "db-content"))
    tag Brand    EVGElement (props (className "db-brand") (role "button"))
    tag BrandMk  EVGElement (props (className "db-brand-mark") (a11yHidden true))
    tag BrandMkT EVGElement (props (className "db-brand-mark-text") (elementType 1))
    tag BrandTx  EVGElement (props (className "db-brand-text") (elementType 1))
    tag SideBody EVGElement (props (className "db-side-body"))
    tag SideGrp  EVGElement (props (className "db-side-group"))
    tag GrpLbl   EVGElement (props (className "db-group-label") (elementType 1))
    tag Menu     EVGElement (props (className "db-menu") (role "list"))
    tag MenuItem EVGElement (props (className "db-menu-item") (role "listitem"))
    tag QuickRow EVGElement (props (className "db-quick-row"))
    tag Quick    EVGElement (props (className "db-quick") (role "button"))
    tag QuickIc  EVGElement (props (className "db-quick-icon") (tagName "path") (a11yHidden true))
    tag QuickTx  EVGElement (props (className "db-quick-text") (elementType 1))
    tag IconBtn  EVGElement (props (className "db-iconbtn") (role "button"))
    tag IconBtnI EVGElement (props (className "db-iconbtn-icon") (tagName "path") (a11yHidden true))
    ; A LINK, not a button. The difference is what a reader is told will
    ; happen: a link goes somewhere, a button does something here.
    tag NavItem   EVGElement (props (className "db-nav") (role "link"))
    tag NavItemOn EVGElement (props (className "db-nav db-nav-on") (role "link"))
    tag NavIc    EVGElement (props (className "db-nav-icon") (tagName "path") (a11yHidden true))
    tag NavTx    EVGElement (props (className "db-nav-text") (elementType 1))
    tag NavTxOn  EVGElement (props (className "db-nav-text-on") (elementType 1))
    tag NavTxDim EVGElement (props (className "db-nav-text-dim") (elementType 1))
    tag User     EVGElement (props (className "db-user") (role "button"))
    tag Avatar   EVGElement (props (className "db-avatar") (a11yHidden true))
    tag AvatarTx EVGElement (props (className "db-avatar-text") (elementType 1))
    tag UserTx   EVGElement (props (className "db-user-text"))
    tag UserName EVGElement (props (className "db-user-name") (elementType 1))
    tag UserMail EVGElement (props (className "db-user-mail") (elementType 1))

    ; --- the scrolling main region --------------------------------------------
    ; A real scroll container: `overflow: hidden` with a definite height, which
    ; in EVG is now a box whose content keeps its own size and is moved rather
    ; than squeezed. Its `scrollTop` is set from `scrollY` before the layout and
    ; read back after, because the layout is the only thing that knows how far
    ; there is to go.
    tag Scroll   EVGElement (props (className "db-scroll") (role "region") (a11yLabel "Main content"))
    ; The bar is DRAWN, not built: see `displayListJson`.
    ;
    ; --- the views ------------------------------------------------------------
    tag View     EVGElement (props (className "db-view"))
    ; NOT `Head`. That name was already the table's `<thead>` — a second `tag
    ; Head` two hundred lines further down silently won, the table's head
    ; became a plain div, and the rowgroup it had been went out of the
    ; accessible tree with it. The picture was identical; a gate that counts
    ; row groups is what noticed.
    tag ViewHd   EVGElement (props (className "db-head"))
    tag ViewTx   EVGElement (props (className "db-head-title") (elementType 1) (role "heading") (a11yLevel 1))
    tag ViewSub  EVGElement (props (className "db-head-sub") (elementType 1))

    tag Panel    EVGElement (props (className "db-panel"))
    tag PanelHd  EVGElement (props (className "db-panel-head"))
    tag PanelTx  EVGElement (props (className "db-panel-title") (elementType 1) (role "heading") (a11yLevel 2))
    tag PanelSub EVGElement (props (className "db-panel-sub") (elementType 1))
    tag PanelBd  EVGElement (props (className "db-panel-body") (role "list"))

    tag Item     EVGElement (props (className "db-item") (role "listitem"))
    tag ItemMark EVGElement (props (className "db-item-mark"))
    tag ItemMkTx EVGElement (props (className "db-item-mark-text") (elementType 1))
    tag ItemTx   EVGElement (props (className "db-item-text"))
    tag ItemName EVGElement (props (className "db-item-name") (elementType 1))
    tag ItemSub  EVGElement (props (className "db-item-sub") (elementType 1))
    tag ItemTag  EVGElement (props (className "db-item-tag"))
    tag ItemTagT EVGElement (props (className "db-item-tag-text") (elementType 1))

    tag Grid     EVGElement (props (className "db-grid") (role "list"))
    tag Tile     EVGElement (props (className "db-tile") (role "listitem"))
    tag TileTx   EVGElement (props (className "db-tile-title") (elementType 1))
    tag TileSub  EVGElement (props (className "db-tile-sub") (elementType 1))
    tag Bar      EVGElement (props (className "db-bar"))
    tag BarFill  EVGElement (props (className "db-bar-fill"))
    tag BarTx    EVGElement (props (className "db-bar-text") (elementType 1))
}

; One of the four figures across the top.
class DbCard {
    def tid:string ""
    def label:string ""
    def value:string ""
    def delta:string ""
    def up:boolean true
    def trend:string ""
    def sub:string ""

    Constructor () {
    }
}

class DashboardDemo {

    def cards:[DbCard]
    ; Ninety days of visitors. Generated rather than pasted, so the range
    ; buttons have something real to narrow.
    def visitors:[int]
    def range:RadioGroupCtl (new RadioGroupCtl())
    ; The table owns the ORDER, the selection and the sort; the sortable owns
    ; the gesture. See the header for why that is one owner and not two.
    def table:TableCtl (new TableCtl())
    def order:SortableCtl (new SortableCtl())
    def tabs:RadioGroupCtl (new RadioGroupCtl())
    ; What makes the table able to hold a hundred thousand rows: it decides
    ; WHICH rows are worth building, and the answer is about a dozen whatever
    ; the count is. Measured against @tanstack/virtual-core.
    def virt:VirtualCtl (new VirtualCtl())
    ; What a row is about, keyed the way the records are — the badge columns
    ; are not strings the table sorts on, they are pictures of one.
    ; What the Documents pages list. One table of data behind four links —
    ; see `documentsView`.
    def docTitles:[string]
    def docKinds:[string]
    def docWhen:[string]
    def sectionOf:[string]
    def statusOf:[string]
    def reviewerOf:[string]

    def root@(optional):EVGElement
    def sheet@(optional):EVGStyleSheet
    ; The sheet's source, kept so the inspector can show and edit the thing the
    ; app was actually given rather than a reconstruction of it.
    def cssText:string ""
    def layout@(optional):EVGLayout
    ; Whether that layout is of the tree that is there NOW. Set by every
    ; rebuild, cleared by the layout that answers it.
    def layoutStale:boolean true

    def hovered:string ""
    def pressed:string ""
    def focused:string ""
    ; Which sidebar link is the page you are on. One string, because
    ; `aria-current="page"` is a claim about exactly one link.
    def navPage:string "dashboard"

    ; WHICH SET OF COLOURS. One string, handed to `applyTree`, and the whole
    ; of the theme mechanism on this side: the sheet holds the palettes and the
    ; app holds the name of the one it is showing. "" is the sheet's unscoped
    ; rules, which is the shadcn light theme this page was built as; "marine"
    ; is the block at the foot of `dashboard.css`.
    ;
    ; A theme may only override properties the unscoped rule already declares —
    ; see the note there — so leaving one puts the original value back without
    ; anything having to remember what it was.
    def theme:string ""

    ; The page got WIDER when the sidebar arrived, rather than the content
    ; getting narrower. 1336 = 255 sidebar + 1 hairline + 1080, so `db-main`
    ; keeps its 1032 and the chart keeps its 994 — and every measured label
    ; position under it is the number it already was.
    def pageW:double 1336.0
    ; A VIEWPORT height now, not a document height. The dashboard's own content
    ; is about 1370 tall and the page is 900, which is the whole point: the
    ; sidebar stands still and the content moves under it.
    def pageH:double 900.0
    ; How far the main region is scrolled. Written here, applied to the element
    ; before the layout runs and read back after — the layout clamps it, and it
    ; is the only thing that knows what to clamp it to.
    def scrollY:double 0.0
    ; Which slice of the last display list is the CHART's.
    ;
    ; The gate used to tell the chart's commands from the page's by saying
    ; "nothing in the element tree draws a path, so every path is Vela's". That
    ; was true until the sidebar's icons became real SVG artwork, and then it
    ; was silently wrong — the chart's bounding box grew to include a folder
    ; icon 260 pixels to its left. So the demo says where the chart is instead
    ; of the gate guessing, because the demo is the only thing that knows.
    def chartFrom:int 0
    def chartTo:int 0

    ; --- the surface's ripple --------------------------------------------------
    ;
    ; Where the page was touched and how long ago. `evg-surface-effect: ripple`
    ; is in the sheet — the effect and its shape are STYLE — and these three
    ; are state, like `scrollTop`: a stylesheet has no business owning where
    ; somebody clicked.
    ;
    ; A negative age means nothing is in flight, which is the resting state and
    ; costs the renderer one comparison.
    ; EVERY touch still in flight, oldest first. Three parallel arrays because
    ; that is the shape the uniform wants at the other end.
    def dropXs:[double]
    def dropYs:[double]
    def dropAges:[double]
    ; Where the last drop was left, so a finger dragged across the surface
    ; leaves a TRAIL rather than one ring that follows it about. -1 means the
    ; pointer is not down.
    def lastDropX:double -1.0
    def lastDropY:double -1.0
    ; The chart's drawn size. ONE owner: the spec is built from these numbers
    ; and the placeholder box is set to them inline, so the box the layout
    ; reserves and the picture Vela computes cannot disagree.
    ;
    ; `chartW` is the CARD's inner width — 1032 less two 18px paddings and two
    ; 1px borders — because a chart that stops short of the card it is in reads
    ; as a mistake rather than as a margin.
    def chartW:double 994.0
    def chartH:double 300.0
    ; Room for the labels, which Vela draws OUTSIDE the plot: the y ticks to
    ; the left of x=0 and the x ticks below y=height. The command coordinates
    ; put the plot's corner at the origin, so emitting at the box's corner
    ; hangs the y labels off its left edge — measured, not guessed: geometry
    ; came back spanning 43..938 while "600" and "400" drew at 29.
    ;
    ; So the plot is inset by the gutter and shrunk by it, and the two numbers
    ; are stated once here rather than folded into the spec.
    def gutterX:double 46.0
    def gutterY:double 34.0
    ; The table's scrollable box. ONE owner, handed to `VirtualCtl` and set on
    ; the body inline, because the height the virtualiser reasons about and the
    ; height the layout gives the box have to be the same number.
    def tableViewport:double 414.0

    Constructor () {
    }

    ; --- the data -------------------------------------------------------------

    ; A deterministic wobble. Not random: a demo whose picture changes every
    ; run has nothing a gate can assert about it.
    fn buildSeries:void () {
        def i:int 0
        while (i < 90) {
            def base:double (220.0 + ((to_double i) * 2.4))
            def wobble:double (((to_double ((i * 37) % 61)) - 30.0) * 3.1)
            def weekly:double (((to_double ((i * 53) % 7)) - 3.0) * 14.0)
            def v:double ((base + wobble) + weekly)
            if (v < 40.0) {
                v = 40.0
            }
            push visitors (to_int v)
            i = (i + 1)
        }
    }

    fn rangeDays:int () {
        if (range.value == "d7") {
            return 7
        }
        if (range.value == "d30") {
            return 30
        }
        return 90
    }

    fn rangeLabel:string () {
        if (range.value == "d7") {
            return "Total for the last 7 days"
        }
        if (range.value == "d30") {
            return "Total for the last 30 days"
        }
        return "Total for the last 3 months"
    }

    ; --- the specification ----------------------------------------------------

    ; Vega, built here rather than read from a file, because the number of
    ; points depends on the range button and a chart that cannot change is not
    ; a chart this page needs.
    fn specText:string () {
        def days:int (this.rangeDays())
        def first:int (90 - days)
        def rows:string ""
        def i:int first
        while (i < 90) {
            if (i > first) {
                rows = (rows + ",")
            }
            rows = (((((rows + "{\"d\":") + (to_string (i - first))) + ",\"v\":") + (to_string (itemAt visitors i))) + "}")
            i = (i + 1)
        }

        def s:string "{"
        s = (s + "\"$schema\":\"https://vega.github.io/schema/vega/v6.json\",")
        s = (s + "\"background\":null,\"padding\":0,")
        s = ((((s + "\"width\":") + (to_string (to_int (chartW - gutterX)))) + ",\"height\":") + (to_string (to_int (chartH - gutterY))))
        s = (s + ",\"style\":\"cell\",")
        s = (((s + "\"data\":[{\"name\":\"src\",\"values\":[") + rows) + "]}],")
        s = (s + "\"marks\":[")
        ; The filled body, then the line on top of it. Two marks over one
        ; dataset, which is the shape the reference draws and the thing a
        ; single `AreaChart` component cannot express.
        s = (s + "{\"type\":\"area\",\"from\":{\"data\":\"src\"},\"encode\":{\"update\":{")
        s = (((s + "\"orient\":{\"value\":\"vertical\"},\"fill\":{\"value\":\"") + (this.inkHex("db-ink-area"))) + "\"},\"fillOpacity\":{\"value\":0.55},")
        s = (s + "\"x\":{\"scale\":\"x\",\"field\":\"d\"},\"y\":{\"scale\":\"y\",\"field\":\"v\"},\"y2\":{\"scale\":\"y\",\"value\":0}}}},")
        s = (s + "{\"type\":\"line\",\"from\":{\"data\":\"src\"},\"encode\":{\"update\":{")
        s = (((s + "\"stroke\":{\"value\":\"") + (this.inkHex("db-ink-line"))) + "\"},\"strokeWidth\":{\"value\":1.5},")
        s = (s + "\"x\":{\"scale\":\"x\",\"field\":\"d\"},\"y\":{\"scale\":\"y\",\"field\":\"v\"}}}}")
        s = (s + "],")
        s = (s + "\"scales\":[")
        s = (s + "{\"name\":\"x\",\"type\":\"linear\",\"domain\":{\"data\":\"src\",\"field\":\"d\"},\"range\":[0,{\"signal\":\"width\"}],\"zero\":false},")
        s = (s + "{\"name\":\"y\",\"type\":\"linear\",\"domain\":{\"data\":\"src\",\"field\":\"v\"},\"range\":[{\"signal\":\"height\"},0],\"nice\":true,\"zero\":true}")
        s = (s + "],")
        s = (s + "\"axes\":[")
        s = (((((((s + "{\"scale\":\"x\",\"orient\":\"bottom\",\"grid\":false,\"tickCount\":6,\"labelColor\":\"") + (this.inkHex("db-ink-label"))) + "\",\"labelFontSize\":11,\"domainColor\":\"") + (this.inkHex("db-ink-rule"))) + "\",\"tickColor\":\"") + (this.inkHex("db-ink-rule"))) + "\"},")
        s = (((((s + "{\"scale\":\"y\",\"orient\":\"left\",\"grid\":true,\"tickCount\":4,\"labelColor\":\"") + (this.inkHex("db-ink-label"))) + "\",\"labelFontSize\":11,\"domain\":false,\"ticks\":false,\"gridColor\":\"") + (this.inkHex("db-ink-grid"))) + "\"}")
        s = (s + "]}")
        return s
    }

    ; --- setup ----------------------------------------------------------------

    fn addCard:DbCard (tid:string label:string value:string delta:string up:boolean trend:string sub:string) {
        def c (new DbCard())
        c.tid = tid
        c.label = label
        c.value = value
        c.delta = delta
        c.up = up
        c.trend = trend
        c.sub = sub
        push cards c
        return c
    }

    fn init:void (css:string) {
        def s (new EVGStyleSheet())
        s.parse(css)
        sheet = s
        cssText = css

        this.buildSeries()

        this.addCard("db-revenue" "Total Revenue" "$1,250.00" "+12.5%" true
            "Trending up this month" "Visitors for the last 6 months")
        this.addCard("db-customers" "New Customers" "1,234" "-20%" false
            "Down 20% this period" "Acquisition needs attention")
        this.addCard("db-accounts" "Active Accounts" "45,678" "+12.5%" true
            "Strong user retention" "Engagement exceed targets")
        this.addCard("db-growth" "Growth Rate" "4.5%" "+4.5%" true
            "Steady performance increase" "Meets growth projections")

        this.buildTable()
        this.addDoc("Q3 revenue extract" "Dataset" "Updated 2 days ago")
        this.addDoc("Churn cohort 2025" "Dataset" "Updated 6 days ago")
        this.addDoc("Support volume by week" "Dataset" "Updated 3 weeks ago")
        this.addDoc("Quarterly business review" "Report" "Generated 1 day ago")
        this.addDoc("Pipeline forecast" "Report" "Generated 5 days ago")
        this.addDoc("Renewal risk" "Report" "Generated 2 weeks ago")
        this.addDoc("Cover letter, EMR bid" "Draft" "Written 4 hours ago")
        this.addDoc("Executive summary, v3" "Draft" "Written yesterday")

        ; A RADIO GROUP, not a toggle group. The two differ in one measured
        ; way: clicking the selected item of a toggle group DESELECTS it, and
        ; a range picker with no range picked is a chart with nothing to draw.
        range.tid = "db-range"
        range.name = "Range"
        range.addItem("d90" "Last 3 months" false)
        range.addItem("d30" "Last 30 days" false)
        range.addItem("d7" "Last 7 days" false)
        range.value = "d90"
        range.build()

        tabs.tid = "db-tabs"
        tabs.name = "Sections"
        tabs.addItem("outline" "Outline" false)
        tabs.addItem("past" "Past Performance" false)
        tabs.addItem("people" "Key Personnel" false)
        tabs.addItem("focus" "Focus Documents" false)
        tabs.value = "outline"
        tabs.build()

        focused = "db-range-d90"
        this.rebuild()
    }

    ; --- the table's data -----------------------------------------------------

    fn addDoc:void (title:string kind:string when:string) {
        push docTitles title
        push docKinds kind
        push docWhen when
    }

    fn addSection:void (key:string header:string section:string status:string target:string limit:string reviewer:string) {
        def cells:[string]
        push cells header
        push cells section
        push cells status
        push cells target
        push cells limit
        push cells reviewer
        table.addRecord(key cells)
        push sectionOf section
        push statusOf status
        push reviewerOf reviewer
    }

    ; The sortable holds ONLY THE ROWS ON SCREEN, rebuilt when the range moves.
    ;
    ; The first version gave it an item per record. Five thousand items, and a
    ; drop then looked each one up in the records by key — five thousand times
    ; a linear search, twenty-five million comparisons for one drag. That is
    ; the shape of the bug virtualisation exists to prevent, written into the
    ; thing meant to demonstrate it.
    ;
    ; And it is not a compromise: a row that cannot be seen cannot be dragged.
    ; The gesture is over what is on screen, so that is what the sortable is
    ; given, and a drop moves ONE record in the full list — a splice, not a
    ; rebuild.
    fn syncOrder:void () {
        def keep:[UiItem]
        order.items = keep
        def rows:[UiRecord] (table.sortedRecords())
        def first:int (virt.firstVisible())
        def last:int (virt.lastVisible())
        if (first < 0) {
            order.build()
            return
        }
        def i:int first
        while (i <= last) {
            if (i < (array_length rows)) {
                def r:UiRecord (itemAt rows i)
                order.addItem(r.key (itemAt r.cells 0) false)
            }
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        order.build()
    }

    fn buildTable:void () {
        table.tid = "db-table"
        table.name = "Document sections"
        table.addColumn("header" "Header" false true)
        table.addColumn("type" "Section Type" false true)
        table.addColumn("status" "Status" false true)
        table.addColumn("target" "Target" true true)
        table.addColumn("limit" "Limit" true true)
        ; The reviewer column does not sort. Half its cells are a NAME and half
        ; are an empty select, and sorting a column where "unassigned" has no
        ; value is a claim about where those rows belong that nothing supports.
        table.addColumn("reviewer" "Reviewer" false false)

        order.tid = "db-order"
        order.name = "Section order"

        this.addSection("cover" "Cover page" "Cover page" "In Process" "18" "5" "Eddie Lake")
        this.addSection("toc" "Table of contents" "Table of contents" "Done" "29" "24" "Eddie Lake")
        this.addSection("exec" "Executive summary" "Narrative" "Done" "10" "13" "Eddie Lake")
        this.addSection("tech" "Technical approach" "Narrative" "Done" "27" "23" "Jamik Tashpulatov")
        this.addSection("design" "Design" "Narrative" "In Process" "2" "16" "Jamik Tashpulatov")
        this.addSection("caps" "Capabilities" "Narrative" "In Process" "20" "8" "Jamik Tashpulatov")
        this.addSection("integ" "Integration with existing systems" "Narrative" "In Process" "19" "21" "Jamik Tashpulatov")
        ; The last two are unassigned, which is what the Reviewer column's
        ; select is for and the reason that column does not sort.
        this.addSection("innov" "Innovation and Advantages" "Narrative" "Done" "25" "26" "")
        this.addSection("emr" "Overview of EMR's Innovative Solutions" "Technical content" "Done" "7" "23" "")
        ; And then a great many more, because a virtualiser that is only ever
        ; asked for nine rows proves nothing. These are generated rather than
        ; written out: the point is the COUNT, and the nine above are the ones
        ; the reference actually shows.
        def i:int 0
        while (i < 4991) {
            def n:int (i + 10)
            def status:string "In Process"
            if ((n % 3) == 0) {
                status = "Done"
            }
            def reviewer:string "Eddie Lake"
            if ((n % 4) == 0) {
                reviewer = "Jamik Tashpulatov"
            }
            if ((n % 7) == 0) {
                reviewer = ""
            }
            this.addSection(("s" + (to_string n)) (("Section " + (to_string n))) "Narrative" status
                (to_string ((n * 7) % 40)) (to_string ((n * 13) % 40)) reviewer)
            i = (i + 1)
        }

        ; NO PAGER. Virtualisation replaces it rather than sitting inside it,
        ; and the first version had them fighting: the virtualiser indexed into
        ; all five thousand records while `pageRecords` handed back a thousand,
        ; so scrolling past row 999 built five rows and then nothing. Two
        ; mechanisms that both decide which rows exist is one too many.
        ;
        ; So the page is the whole table and the scroll box is what a person
        ; moves through — which is also what the reference does, and why its
        ; table scrolls rather than paginating.
        table.pageSize = (array_length table.records)
        virt.tid = "db-virt"
        virt.rowHeight = 46.0
        virt.viewport = tableViewport
        ; Two rows either side, so a scroll of one row never exposes a strip
        ; of nothing before the next frame.
        virt.overscan = 2
        virt.count = (array_length table.records)

        ; NOT `table.build()`. `TableCtl` can build its own display tree and
        ; this demo does not use it — it draws the table itself, because the
        ; reference's cells are badges and selects rather than strings. With
        ; five thousand records that call builds five thousand EVG rows, every
        ; press, for nothing: the gate went from a second to a timeout, and the
        ; virtualiser it was supposed to be testing was the only thing on the
        ; page NOT doing the wasteful thing.
        order.build()
    }

    ; What a record holds for a column, by key — the demo draws badges from
    ; these rather than from the cell strings, so the picture and the value a
    ; sort uses are the same number seen two ways.
    fn cellOf:string (r:UiRecord key:string) {
        def at:int (table.columnAt(key))
        if (at < 0) {
            return ""
        }
        if (at >= (array_length r.cells)) {
            return ""
        }
        return (itemAt r.cells at)
    }

    ; --- the tree -------------------------------------------------------------

    ; --- the sidebar's icons, verbatim from lucide-static 1.37.0 (ISC) --------
    ;
    ; REAL ARTWORK, and the reason is a bug report from a phone: the icons used
    ; to be text glyphs — "●", "▭", "◕" — and they sat low against
    ; the words beside them. Two causes, and only one of them was a mistake.
    ;
    ; The mistake was two numbers where there should have been one: the icon
    ; box was 18 tall and the label 20, `align-items: center` centres the
    ; BOXES, and a text box's baseline is measured from its own top. One pixel,
    ; every row. A browser does the same with the same declarations.
    ;
    ; The other cause was not fixable that way. A glyph sits on a BASELINE, and
    ; where its ink lands above that baseline is the type designer's business:
    ; measured at 13px, the ink of "●" and "▭" centres a further pixel
    ; below the ink of a capital letter, while "↺" centres exactly on it —
    ; which is why the Lifecycle row looked right in the same screenshot where
    ; Team and Projects looked wrong. Centring a glyph OPTICALLY needs its ink
    ; box, and `EVGTextMeasurer` estimates.
    ;
    ; So the icons stop being text. A 16x16 `path` element is a BOX, centred by
    ; the row like any other box, with no baseline anywhere in the arrangement —
    ; which is also exactly what the reference does, because lucide ships SVG.
    ; The mechanism is the timeline demo's, unchanged.
    sfn svgOpen:string () {
        return "<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 24 24' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' stroke-width='2' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'>"
    }

    ; layout-dashboard
    sfn icoDashboard:string () {
        return ((DashboardDemo.svgOpen()) + "<rect width='7' height='9' x='3' y='3' rx='1'/><rect width='7' height='5' x='14' y='3' rx='1'/><rect width='7' height='9' x='14' y='12' rx='1'/><rect width='7' height='5' x='3' y='16' rx='1'/></svg>")
    }
    ; list-checks
    sfn icoLifecycle:string () {
        return ((DashboardDemo.svgOpen()) + "<path d='M13 5h8'/><path d='M13 12h8'/><path d='M13 19h8'/><path d='m3 17 2 2 4-4'/><path d='m3 7 2 2 4-4'/></svg>")
    }
    ; chart-column
    sfn icoAnalytics:string () {
        return ((DashboardDemo.svgOpen()) + "<path d='M3 3v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h16'/><path d='M18 17V9'/><path d='M13 17V5'/><path d='M8 17v-3'/></svg>")
    }
    ; folder
    sfn icoProjects:string () {
        return ((DashboardDemo.svgOpen()) + "<path d='M20 20a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8a2 2 0 0 0-2-2h-7.9a2 2 0 0 1-1.69-.9L9.6 3.9A2 2 0 0 0 7.93 3H4a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v13a2 2 0 0 0 2 2Z'/></svg>")
    }
    ; users
    sfn icoTeam:string () {
        return ((DashboardDemo.svgOpen()) + "<path d='M16 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H6a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2'/><path d='M16 3.128a4 4 0 0 1 0 7.744'/><path d='M22 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-3-3.87'/><circle cx='9' cy='7' r='4'/></svg>")
    }
    ; database
    sfn icoLibrary:string () {
        return ((DashboardDemo.svgOpen()) + "<ellipse cx='12' cy='5' rx='9' ry='3'/><path d='M3 5V19A9 3 0 0 0 21 19V5'/><path d='M3 12A9 3 0 0 0 21 12'/></svg>")
    }
    ; file-text
    sfn icoReports:string () {
        return ((DashboardDemo.svgOpen()) + "<path d='M6 22a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h8a2.4 2.4 0 0 1 1.704.706l3.588 3.588A2.4 2.4 0 0 1 20 8v12a2 2 0 0 1-2 2z'/><path d='M14 2v5a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h5'/><path d='M10 9H8'/><path d='M16 13H8'/><path d='M16 17H8'/></svg>")
    }
    ; file-type-2
    sfn icoAssistant:string () {
        return ((DashboardDemo.svgOpen()) + "<path d='M12 22h6a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8a2.4 2.4 0 0 0-.706-1.706l-3.588-3.588A2.4 2.4 0 0 0 14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v6'/><path d='M14 2v5a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h5'/><path d='M3 16v-1.5a.5.5 0 0 1 .5-.5h7a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5V16'/><path d='M6 22h2'/><path d='M7 14v8'/></svg>")
    }
    ; ellipsis
    sfn icoMore:string () {
        return ((DashboardDemo.svgOpen()) + "<circle cx='12' cy='12' r='1'/><circle cx='19' cy='12' r='1'/><circle cx='5' cy='12' r='1'/></svg>")
    }
    ; circle-plus
    sfn icoCreate:string () {
        return ((DashboardDemo.svgOpen()) + "<circle cx='12' cy='12' r='10'/><path d='M8 12h8'/><path d='M12 8v8'/></svg>")
    }
    ; mail
    sfn icoMail:string () {
        return ((DashboardDemo.svgOpen()) + "<path d='m22 7-8.991 5.727a2 2 0 0 1-2.009 0L2 7'/><rect x='2' y='4' width='20' height='16' rx='2'/></svg>")
    }

    ; --- the sidebar ----------------------------------------------------------

    ; One link. The fill and `aria-current` are set from the SAME test, because
    ; a highlighted row that does not say it is current, or a current one that
    ; is not highlighted, is one of the two audiences being told the truth.
    fn navRow:EVGElement (key:string label:string icon:string dim:boolean) {
        def id:string ("db-nav-" + key)
        def on:boolean (navPage == key)
        def item (tree Db (MenuItem))
        def row (tree Db (NavItem (props (id id) (a11yLabel label))))
        if on {
            row = (tree Db (NavItemOn (props (id id) (a11yLabel label))))
        }
        row.a11yFocusable = true
        if on {
            row.a11yCurrent = "page"
        }
        def ic:EVGElement (tree Db (NavIc))
        ic.svgSource = icon
        row.addChild(ic)
        if on {
            row.addChild((tree Db (NavTxOn label)))
        } {
            if dim {
                row.addChild((tree Db (NavTxDim label)))
            } {
                row.addChild((tree Db (NavTx label)))
            }
        }
        item.addChild(row)
        return item
    }

    ; The reference's sidebar: a brand, an action, two groups of links and the
    ; account at the bottom.
    ;
    ; --- WHY THE HAIRLINE IS AN ELEMENT ---------------------------------------
    ;
    ; It wants to be `border-right: 1px solid #e4e4e7` on `.db-side`, and that
    ; line would do NOTHING. `setAttribute` has branches for `border`,
    ; `border-width`, `border-color` and `border-radius` and none for the four
    ; sides; the `borderTopWidth`..`borderLeftWidth` fields exist on the element
    ; and are read by no renderer, because the box model has one border width
    ; and spends it on all four sides. No style error either — the stylesheet
    ; reports selectors it cannot parse, not declarations it cannot use.
    ;
    ; So a one-pixel column, which is a true statement about a missing feature
    ; rather than a trick to hide one. `border-bottom` in this very sheet and in
    ; profile.css is dropped the same silent way and has been all along.
    fn sidebar:EVGElement () {
        def side (tree Db (Side (props (id "db-side"))))

        def brand (tree Db (Brand (props (id "db-brand") (a11yLabel "Acme Inc."))))
        brand.a11yFocusable = true
        ; It opens a team switcher in the reference, so it says so. A button
        ; that silently turns out to be a menu is a surprise a reader gets
        ; after committing to the press.
        brand.a11yHasPopup = "menu"
        brand.a11yExpanded = (EVGA11yTri.no())
        def mark (tree Db (BrandMk))
        mark.addChild((tree Db (BrandMkT "A")))
        brand.addChild(mark)
        brand.addChild((tree Db (BrandTx "Acme Inc.")))
        side.addChild(brand)

        def body (tree Db (SideBody))

        def g1 (tree Db (SideGrp))
        ; The action pair. BUTTONS, both of them, and not part of the list
        ; below: "Quick Create" goes nowhere, it does something.
        def qrow (tree Db (QuickRow))
        def quick (tree Db (Quick (props (id "db-quick") (a11yLabel "Quick Create"))))
        quick.a11yFocusable = true
        def qi:EVGElement (tree Db (QuickIc))
        qi.svgSource = (DashboardDemo.icoCreate())
        quick.addChild(qi)
        quick.addChild((tree Db (QuickTx "Quick Create")))
        qrow.addChild(quick)
        ; An icon with no words. The label is the whole interface for a reader,
        ; which is why it is stated rather than left to the glyph — and the
        ; glyph is hidden, because "envelope" read out after "Inbox" is the
        ; same thing said twice in two vocabularies.
        def inbox (tree Db (IconBtn (props (id "db-inbox") (a11yLabel "Inbox"))))
        inbox.a11yFocusable = true
        def mi:EVGElement (tree Db (IconBtnI))
        mi.svgSource = (DashboardDemo.icoMail())
        inbox.addChild(mi)
        qrow.addChild(inbox)
        g1.addChild(qrow)

        ; No name on this list. The reference draws no label above it, and a
        ; name invented here is a word a reader hears that nobody wrote.
        def m1 (tree Db (Menu (props (id "db-menu-pages"))))
        m1.addChild((this.navRow("dashboard" "Dashboard" (DashboardDemo.icoDashboard()) false)))
        m1.addChild((this.navRow("lifecycle" "Lifecycle" (DashboardDemo.icoLifecycle()) false)))
        m1.addChild((this.navRow("analytics" "Analytics" (DashboardDemo.icoAnalytics()) false)))
        m1.addChild((this.navRow("projects" "Projects" (DashboardDemo.icoProjects()) false)))
        m1.addChild((this.navRow("team" "Team" (DashboardDemo.icoTeam()) false)))
        g1.addChild(m1)
        body.addChild(g1)

        def g2 (tree Db (SideGrp))
        g2.addChild((tree Db (GrpLbl "Documents")))
        ; This one IS named, by the words drawn above it. A group label a
        ; reader cannot associate with the group is a label for nobody.
        def m2 (tree Db (Menu (props (id "db-menu-docs") (a11yLabel "Documents"))))
        m2.addChild((this.navRow("library" "Data Library" (DashboardDemo.icoLibrary()) false)))
        m2.addChild((this.navRow("reports" "Reports" (DashboardDemo.icoReports()) false)))
        m2.addChild((this.navRow("assistant" "Word Assistant" (DashboardDemo.icoAssistant()) false)))
        m2.addChild((this.navRow("more" "More" (DashboardDemo.icoMore()) true)))
        g2.addChild(m2)
        body.addChild(g2)

        ; The body GROWS (see the sheet), so the account row below it sits on
        ; the bottom edge whatever the links above it add up to.
        side.addChild(body)

        def user (tree Db (User (props (id "db-user") (a11yLabel "Account, Tero Tolonen"))))
        user.a11yFocusable = true
        user.a11yHasPopup = "menu"
        user.a11yExpanded = (EVGA11yTri.no())
        def av (tree Db (Avatar))
        av.addChild((tree Db (AvatarTx "TT")))
        user.addChild(av)
        def ut (tree Db (UserTx))
        ut.addChild((tree Db (UserName "Tero Tolonen")))
        ut.addChild((tree Db (UserMail "tero@example.com")))
        user.addChild(ut)
        side.addChild(user)
        return side
    }

    fn cardBody:EVGElement (c:DbCard) {
        def card (tree Db (Card (props (id c.tid) (a11yLabel ((c.label + ", ") + c.value)))))

        def top (tree Db (CardTop))
        top.addChild((tree Db (CardLbl c.label)))
        top.addChild((tree Db (Spacer)))
        def badge (tree Db (Badge))
        if c.up {
            badge = (tree Db (BadgeUp))
        }
        def arrow:string "↘"
        if c.up {
            arrow = "↗"
        }
        badge.addChild((tree Db (Arrow arrow)))
        badge.addChild((tree Db (BadgeTx c.delta)))
        top.addChild(badge)
        card.addChild(top)

        card.addChild((tree Db (Big c.value)))

        def foot (tree Db (Foot))
        def ftop (tree Db (FootTop))
        ftop.addChild((tree Db (Trend c.trend)))
        ftop.addChild((tree Db (Arrow arrow)))
        foot.addChild(ftop)
        foot.addChild((tree Db (Sub c.sub)))
        card.addChild(foot)
        return card
    }

    fn chartCard:EVGElement () {
        def card (tree Db (Chart (props (id "db-chart"))))

        def head (tree Db (ChartHd))
        def ht (tree Db (ChartTx))
        ht.addChild((tree Db (Title "Total Visitors")))
        ht.addChild((tree Db (ChartSub (this.rangeLabel()))))
        head.addChild(ht)
        head.addChild((tree Db (Spacer)))

        def segs (tree Db (Segs (props (id "db-range"))))
        def i:int 0
        while (i < (array_length range.items)) {
            def it:UiItem (itemAt range.items i)
            def on:boolean (range.value == it.value)
            def seg (tree Db (Seg (props (id ("db-range-" + it.value)) (a11yLabel it.name))))
            if on {
                seg = (tree Db (SegOn (props (id ("db-range-" + it.value)) (a11yLabel it.name))))
            }
            seg.a11yFocusable = true
            if on {
                seg.a11yChecked = (EVGA11yTri.yes())
            } {
                seg.a11yChecked = (EVGA11yTri.no())
            }
            seg.addChild((tree Db (SegTx it.name)))
            segs.addChild(seg)
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        head.addChild(segs)
        card.addChild(head)

        ; The placeholder. Its size is set from the SAME two numbers the
        ; specification is built with, inline rather than in the sheet, because
        ; two owners for one number is how the resize demo lost a frame.
        def box (tree Db (ChartBox (props (id "db-chart-box")
            (a11yLabel ("Visitors, " + (this.rangeLabel()))))))
        box.width = (EVGUnit.px(chartW))
        box.height = (EVGUnit.px(chartH))
        card.addChild(box)
        return card
    }

    ; --- the table's tree -----------------------------------------------------

    fn tabStrip:EVGElement () {
        def strip (tree Db (Tabs (props (id "db-tabs"))))
        def i:int 0
        while (i < (array_length tabs.items)) {
            def it:UiItem (itemAt tabs.items i)
            def on:boolean (tabs.value == it.value)
            def count:string ""
            if (it.value == "past") { count = "3" }
            if (it.value == "people") { count = "2" }
            ; The COUNT is part of the tab's name. A pill announced on its own
            ; after "Past Performance" is a number with nothing attached to it.
            def label:string it.name
            if ((strlen count) > 0) {
                label = ((it.name + ", ") + count)
            }
            def tab (tree Db (Tab (props (id ("db-tab-" + it.value)) (a11yLabel label))))
            if on {
                tab = (tree Db (TabOn (props (id ("db-tab-" + it.value)) (a11yLabel label))))
            }
            tab.a11yFocusable = true
            if on {
                tab.a11ySelected = (EVGA11yTri.yes())
            } {
                tab.a11ySelected = (EVGA11yTri.no())
            }
            tab.addChild((tree Db (TabTx it.name)))
            if ((strlen count) > 0) {
                def pill (tree Db (TabPill))
                pill.addChild((tree Db (PillTx count)))
                tab.addChild(pill)
            }
            strip.addChild(tab)
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        return strip
    }

    fn toolRow:EVGElement () {
        def tools (tree Db (Tools))
        def cols (tree Db (Tool (props (id "db-columns") (a11yLabel "Customize columns"))))
        cols.a11yFocusable = true
        cols.a11yHasPopup = "menu"
        cols.a11yExpanded = (EVGA11yTri.no())
        cols.addChild((tree Db (ToolIc "▤")))
        cols.addChild((tree Db (ToolTx "Customize Columns")))
        cols.addChild((tree Db (Chevron "⌄")))
        tools.addChild(cols)
        def add (tree Db (Tool (props (id "db-add") (a11yLabel "Add section"))))
        add.a11yFocusable = true
        add.addChild((tree Db (ToolIc "+")))
        add.addChild((tree Db (ToolTx "Add Section")))
        tools.addChild(add)
        return tools
    }

    fn headRow:EVGElement () {
        def row (tree Db (Row (props (id "db-table-headrow"))))
        ; The header is row 1. Everything below it starts at 2, which is what
        ; `rowIndexOf` is adding.
        row.a11yRowIndex = 1

        ; The select-all box and the drag column's header, both of which have
        ; a job and no words. A `columnheader` with no name is a column a
        ; reader is told about and cannot identify, so each says what it is.
        def selTh (tree Db (Th (props (id "db-table-selectcol") (a11yLabel "Select"))))
        def all (tree Db (Check (props (id "db-table-selectall") (a11yLabel "Select all on this page"))))
        all.a11yFocusable = true
        def st:int (table.selectAllState())
        if (st == 2) {
            all.a11yChecked = (EVGA11yTri.yes())
            all.addChild((tree Db (Tick "✓")))
        } {
            if (st == 3) {
                all.a11yChecked = (EVGA11yTri.mixed())
                all.addChild((tree Db (Tick "–")))
            } {
                all.a11yChecked = (EVGA11yTri.no())
            }
        }
        selTh.addChild(all)
        row.addChild(selTh)
        row.addChild((tree Db (Th (props (id "db-table-dragcol") (a11yLabel "Reorder")))))

        def i:int 0
        while (i < (array_length table.columns)) {
            def c:UiColumn (itemAt table.columns i)
            def th (tree Db (Th (props (id ("db-table-col-" + c.key)) (a11yLabel c.label))))
            th.className = ("db-th db-col-" + c.key)
            if c.sortable {
                th.a11yFocusable = true
                th.a11ySorted = (table.sortStateOf(c.key))
            }
            th.addChild((tree Db (CellTx c.label)))
            row.addChild(th)
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        row.addChild((tree Db (Th (props (id "db-table-menucol") (a11yLabel "Actions")))))
        return row
    }

    fn statusCell:EVGElement (value:string) {
        def done:boolean (value == "Done")
        def chip (tree Db (Status))
        if done {
            chip = (tree Db (StatusD))
        }
        if done {
            chip.addChild((tree Db (StatIcD "✓")))
        } {
            chip.addChild((tree Db (StatIc "◌")))
        }
        chip.addChild((tree Db (StatTx value)))
        return chip
    }

    fn bodyRow:EVGElement (r:UiRecord) {
        def carried:boolean (order.activeValue == r.key)
        def row (tree Db (Row (props (id ("db-table-row-" + r.key)))))
        if r.selected {
            row = (tree Db (RowOn (props (id ("db-table-row-" + r.key)))))
        }
        row.a11ySelected = (EVGA11yTri.no())
        if r.selected {
            row.a11ySelected = (EVGA11yTri.yes())
        }

        def selTd (tree Db (Td (props (id ("db-table-cell-" + r.key) + "-sel"))))
        def box (tree Db (Check (props (id ("db-table-check-" + r.key))
            (a11yLabel ("Select " + (this.cellOf(r "header")))))))
        box.a11yFocusable = true
        if r.selected {
            box.a11yChecked = (EVGA11yTri.yes())
            box.addChild((tree Db (Tick "✓")))
        } {
            box.a11yChecked = (EVGA11yTri.no())
        }
        selTd.addChild(box)
        row.addChild(selTd)

        ; The drag handle. A BUTTON, because that is what dnd-kit makes it and
        ; the only way a keyboard reaches a reorder at all: space picks the row
        ; up, the arrows move it, space drops it and escape puts it back.
        def dragTd (tree Db (Td))
        def grip (tree Db (Grip (props (id ("db-order-item-" + r.key))
            (a11yLabel ("Reorder " + (this.cellOf(r "header")))))))
        grip.a11yFocusable = true
        grip.a11yRoleDescription = "sortable"
        grip.a11yDescription = (SortableCtl.dragInstructions())
        if carried {
            grip.a11yPressed = (EVGA11yTri.yes())
        }
        grip.addChild((tree Db (GripIc "⠿")))
        dragTd.addChild(grip)
        row.addChild(dragTd)

        def i:int 0
        while (i < (array_length table.columns)) {
            def c:UiColumn (itemAt table.columns i)
            def value:string (this.cellOf(r c.key))
            def td (tree Db (Td (props (id (((("db-table-cell-" + r.key) + "-") + c.key))))))
            td.className = ("db-td db-col-" + c.key)
            if (c.key == "header") {
                td.addChild((tree Db (Strong value)))
            } {
                if (c.key == "type") {
                    def chip (tree Db (Chip))
                    chip.addChild((tree Db (ChipTx value)))
                    td.addChild(chip)
                } {
                    if (c.key == "status") {
                        td.addChild((this.statusCell(value)))
                    } {
                        if (c.key == "reviewer") {
                            if ((strlen value) == 0) {
                                def sel (tree Db (Assign (props (id ("db-assign-" + r.key))
                                    (a11yLabel ("Assign a reviewer to " + (this.cellOf(r "header")))))))
                                sel.a11yFocusable = true
                                sel.a11yHasPopup = "listbox"
                                sel.a11yExpanded = (EVGA11yTri.no())
                                sel.addChild((tree Db (AssignT "Assign reviewer")))
                                sel.addChild((tree Db (Chevron "⌄")))
                                td.addChild(sel)
                            } {
                                td.addChild((tree Db (CellTx value)))
                            }
                        } {
                            td.addChild((tree Db (CellTx value)))
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            row.addChild(td)
            i = (i + 1)
        }

        def menuTd (tree Db (Td))
        def more (tree Db (More (props (id ("db-more-" + r.key))
            (a11yLabel ("Actions for " + (this.cellOf(r "header")))))))
        more.a11yFocusable = true
        more.a11yHasPopup = "menu"
        more.a11yExpanded = (EVGA11yTri.no())
        more.addChild((tree Db (MoreIc "⋮")))
        menuTd.addChild(more)
        row.addChild(menuTd)
        return row
    }

    fn tableCard:EVGElement () {
        def card (tree Db (Chart (props (id "db-table-card"))))

        def head (tree Db (ChartHd))
        head.addChild((this.tabStrip()))
        head.addChild((tree Db (Spacer)))
        head.addChild((this.toolRow()))
        card.addChild(head)

        def t (tree Db (Table (props (id "db-table"))))
        t.a11yRowCount = (virt.rowCount())
        def thead (tree Db (Head))
        thead.addChild((this.headRow()))
        t.addChild(thead)
        ; THE VIRTUALISED BODY, and it is NOT built the way a DOM one is.
        ;
        ; A browser virtualiser puts the WHOLE content height in the document —
        ; a spacer above the range and another below — and lets the scroll
        ; container do the moving. That is how the scrollbar gets its size, and
        ; it works because a browser has a scrolling viewport.
        ;
        ; EVG has no scrolling viewport. `overflow: hidden` clips; nothing
        ; scrolls. So a 230,000-pixel spacer inside a 414-pixel flex column is
        ; not a spacer, it is content that overflows — and the layout does
        ; exactly what it says it does with overflowing fixed children, which
        ; is shrink them all to fit. The first version drew fourteen rows on
        ; top of each other in one 46-pixel line, and it looked for all the
        ; world like a stylesheet bug.
        ;
        ; So the offset is applied instead of the spacers: the window holds
        ; only the rows in range and is slid up by the part of the FIRST row
        ; that is scrolled past — at most one row's height, never a number the
        ; layout has to be talked out of. `virt.totalSize()` is still the
        ; honest content height and is what a scrollbar would be sized from; it
        ; simply never enters the tree.
        def tbody (tree Db (Body (props (id "db-tbody"))))
        tbody.height = (EVGUnit.px(tableViewport))
        ; NO `aria-rowcount` here. It went on the body as well as the table at
        ; first, on the reasoning that the body is what holds the rows — and
        ; ARIA does not allow it on `rowgroup`, which owns no count. Neither
        ; the lint nor the gate could see that: they check the fields a node
        ; carries, not whether its ROLE is allowed to carry them. axe did, the
        ; first time this page was put in front of it. The count is on
        ; `db-table`, which is the element the attribute belongs to.

        ; The SORTED list, which is what the virtualiser's indices are into.
        def rows:[UiRecord] (table.sortedRecords())
        def first:int (virt.firstVisible())
        def last:int (virt.lastVisible())
        if (first < 0) {
            first = 0
            last = (0 - 1)
        }

        ; The sortable is given the rows on screen before they are drawn, so a
        ; grip's tid and the item it stands for cannot disagree.
        this.syncOrder()

        def win (tree Db (Window (props (id "db-window"))))
        ; Zero at the top, negative once scrolled: how much of the first built
        ; row is above the viewport's edge.
        win.top = (EVGUnit.px(((virt.paddingTop()) - virt.scrollOffset)))
        def i:int first
        while (i <= last) {
            if (i < (array_length rows)) {
                def row:EVGElement (this.bodyRow((itemAt rows i)))
                ; WHERE THIS ROW REALLY IS. 1-based and counting the header,
                ; which is what an HTML table's own indices are — a built row
                ; numbered 1..12 would tell a reader they were at the top of
                ; the table four thousand rows down.
                row.a11yRowIndex = (virt.rowIndexOf(i))
                win.addChild(row)
            }
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        tbody.addChild(win)
        t.addChild(tbody)
        card.addChild(t)

        ; What dnd-kit says out loud while a row is being carried. A live
        ; region, because a reorder a reader cannot hear is a reorder that did
        ; not happen as far as they are concerned.
        card.addChild((tree Db (Live (props (id "db-live")) (order.said()))))
        return card
    }

    ; --- the views ------------------------------------------------------------
    ;
    ; Nine links and six builders, which is a claim and not a shortcut: the
    ; four Documents links and More differ by WHAT THEY LIST and by nothing
    ; else, so they share a builder and are told apart by an argument. Writing
    ; five near-identical page functions would have made them look different in
    ; the source and identical on the screen, which is the wrong way round.

    ; The page's h1 and a panel's h2 both need an ID. Without one the a11y
    ; builder invents `<nearest ancestor with an id>/heading@0` for each, and
    ; two headings whose nearest identified ancestor is the same scroll region
    ; get the SAME invented id — a duplicate the lint catches and a screen
    ; reader would have no way to tell apart.
    fn headOf:EVGElement (title:string sub:string) {
        def h (tree Db (ViewHd))
        def t:EVGElement (tree Db (ViewTx title))
        t.id = "db-head-title"
        h.addChild(t)
        h.addChild((tree Db (ViewSub sub)))
        return h
    }

    ; One row of a panel: a mark, a name over a note, and a chip at the end.
    fn itemOf:EVGElement (mark:string name:string sub:string tagText:string) {
        def it (tree Db (Item))
        def mk (tree Db (ItemMark))
        mk.addChild((tree Db (ItemMkTx mark)))
        it.addChild(mk)
        def tx (tree Db (ItemTx))
        tx.addChild((tree Db (ItemName name)))
        tx.addChild((tree Db (ItemSub sub)))
        it.addChild(tx)
        it.addChild((tree Db (Spacer)))
        if ((strlen tagText) > 0) {
            def tg (tree Db (ItemTag))
            tg.addChild((tree Db (ItemTagT tagText)))
            it.addChild(tg)
        }
        return it
    }

    fn panelOf:EVGElement (pid:string title:string sub:string) {
        def p (tree Db (Panel (props (id pid))))
        def hd (tree Db (PanelHd))
        def pt:EVGElement (tree Db (PanelTx title))
        pt.id = (pid + "-title")
        hd.addChild(pt)
        if ((strlen sub) > 0) {
            hd.addChild((tree Db (PanelSub sub)))
        }
        p.addChild(hd)
        p.addChild((tree Db (PanelBd (props (id (pid + "-list")) (a11yLabel title)))))
        return p
    }

    fn panelBody:EVGElement (panel:EVGElement) {
        return (itemAt panel.children 1)
    }

    fn analyticsView:EVGElement () {
        def v (tree Db (View (props (id "db-view"))))
        v.addChild((this.headOf("Analytics" "Traffic and conversion, last 90 days")))
        v.addChild((this.cardRow()))
        v.addChild((this.chartCard()))
        def p:EVGElement (this.panelOf("db-top" "Top pages" "By visitors"))
        def b:EVGElement (this.panelBody(p))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("1" "/pricing" "12,480 visitors" "+18%")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("2" "/docs/getting-started" "9,120 visitors" "+7%")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("3" "/changelog" "6,004 visitors" "-3%")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("4" "/blog/why-we-measure" "4,870 visitors" "+41%")))
        v.addChild(p)
        return v
    }

    fn lifecycleView:EVGElement () {
        def v (tree Db (View (props (id "db-view"))))
        v.addChild((this.headOf("Lifecycle" "Where every proposal is in the process")))
        def p:EVGElement (this.panelOf("db-stages" "Stages" "Six, in order"))
        def b:EVGElement (this.panelBody(p))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("1" "Intake" "12 in this stage" "Done")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("2" "Qualification" "8 in this stage" "Done")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("3" "Drafting" "21 in this stage" "Active")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("4" "Internal review" "5 in this stage" "Active")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("5" "Client review" "3 in this stage" "Waiting")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("6" "Signed" "17 in this stage" "Done")))
        v.addChild(p)
        return v
    }

    fn projectsView:EVGElement () {
        def v (tree Db (View (props (id "db-view"))))
        v.addChild((this.headOf("Projects" "Six running, two of them behind")))
        def g (tree Db (Grid (props (id "db-projects") (a11yLabel "Projects"))))
        g.addChild((this.tileOf("Migration" "Platform" 82)))
        g.addChild((this.tileOf("Cover redesign" "Design" 45)))
        g.addChild((this.tileOf("Quarterly report" "Finance" 96)))
        g.addChild((this.tileOf("Onboarding rewrite" "Docs" 30)))
        g.addChild((this.tileOf("EMR integration" "Platform" 61)))
        g.addChild((this.tileOf("Field research" "Research" 12)))
        v.addChild(g)
        return v
    }

    fn tileOf:EVGElement (title:string kind:string pct:int) {
        def t (tree Db (Tile (props (a11yLabel ((((title + ", ") + kind) + ", ") + ((to_string pct) + "% complete"))))))
        ; Three across, in pixels rather than thirds. 33.33% of 1032 is 344 and
        ; three of those plus two 16-pixel gaps is 1064 — the row wraps to two
        ; and the grid looks like a bug. A percentage is a share of the parent
        ; and knows nothing about the gaps between its siblings.
        t.width = (EVGUnit.px(333.0))
        t.addChild((tree Db (TileTx title)))
        t.addChild((tree Db (TileSub kind)))
        def bar (tree Db (Bar))
        def fill (tree Db (BarFill))
        fill.width = (EVGUnit.percent((to_double pct)))
        bar.addChild(fill)
        t.addChild(bar)
        t.addChild((tree Db (BarTx ((to_string pct) + "% complete"))))
        return t
    }

    fn teamView:EVGElement () {
        def v (tree Db (View (props (id "db-view"))))
        v.addChild((this.headOf("Team" "Six people, and who reviews what")))
        def p:EVGElement (this.panelOf("db-people" "People" ""))
        def b:EVGElement (this.panelBody(p))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("EL" "Eddie Lake" "eddie@example.com" "Reviewer")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("JT" "Jamik Tashpulatov" "jamik@example.com" "Reviewer")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("TT" "Tero Tolonen" "tero@example.com" "Owner")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("AM" "Aino Makela" "aino@example.com" "Editor")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("BN" "Benoit Nadeau" "benoit@example.com" "Editor")))
        b.addChild((this.itemOf("LR" "Luis Ramos" "luis@example.com" "Viewer")))
        v.addChild(p)
        return v
    }

    ; The four Documents links, and the one builder behind them. `only` is the
    ; kind each page shows; "" is More, which shows everything.
    fn documentsView:EVGElement (title:string sub:string only:string) {
        def v (tree Db (View (props (id "db-view"))))
        v.addChild((this.headOf(title sub)))
        def p:EVGElement (this.panelOf("db-docs" title ""))
        def b:EVGElement (this.panelBody(p))
        def i:int 0
        def shown:int 0
        while (i < (array_length docTitles)) {
            def kind:string (itemAt docKinds i)
            def keep:boolean ((strlen only) == 0)
            if (kind == only) {
                keep = true
            }
            if keep {
                b.addChild((this.itemOf("▭" (itemAt docTitles i) (itemAt docWhen i) kind)))
                shown = (shown + 1)
            }
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        if (shown == 0) {
            b.addChild((this.itemOf("◌" "Nothing here yet" "This page has no documents" "")))
        }
        v.addChild(p)
        return v
    }

    fn cardRow:EVGElement () {
        def row (tree Db (Cards (props (id "db-cards"))))
        def i:int 0
        while (i < (array_length cards)) {
            def c:DbCard (itemAt cards i)
            def el:EVGElement (this.cardBody(c))
            el.width = (EVGUnit.percent(25.0))
            row.addChild(el)
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        return row
    }

    fn dashboardView:EVGElement () {
        def main (tree Db (Main (props (id "db-main"))))
        main.addChild((this.cardRow()))
        main.addChild((this.chartCard()))
        main.addChild((this.tableCard()))
        return main
    }

    ; Which page the sidebar is pointing at. One place, so the link that is
    ; marked current and the content that is shown cannot come apart.
    fn viewFor:EVGElement (key:string) {
        if (key == "analytics") { return (this.analyticsView()) }
        if (key == "lifecycle") { return (this.lifecycleView()) }
        if (key == "projects")  { return (this.projectsView()) }
        if (key == "team")      { return (this.teamView()) }
        if (key == "library")   { return (this.documentsView("Data Library" "Everything the team has filed" "Dataset")) }
        if (key == "reports")   { return (this.documentsView("Reports" "Generated, and when" "Report")) }
        if (key == "assistant") { return (this.documentsView("Word Assistant" "Drafts it has written" "Draft")) }
        if (key == "more")      { return (this.documentsView("Documents" "Everything, in one list" "")) }
        return (this.dashboardView())
    }

    fn noteFor:string () {
        if (navPage == "dashboard") {
            return "The chart is a Vega specification run by Vela and painted into this page's display list — geometry and axis labels both. Press a range button, or use the arrows."
        }
        if (navPage == "analytics") {
            return "The same chart, on another page. Press a range button, or use the arrows."
        }
        return "The sidebar navigates: the link marked current and the content shown come from one field. The main region scrolls — Page Down, Page Up, Home, End, or the wheel."
    }

    ; A touch, in page pixels. The click that presses a button is the same
    ; click that starts the ripple: the surface reacts to being touched, and
    ; nothing about the button had to know.
    ; The most drops that can be in flight. Eight is not a number anybody
    ; reaches by tapping — it is what a dragged finger fills in a third of a
    ; second, and the oldest is dropped to make room, which is also the one
    ; that has faded most.
    sfn maxDrops:int () {
        return 8
    }

    fn ripple:void (px:double py:double) {
        push dropXs px
        push dropYs py
        push dropAges 0.0
        lastDropX = px
        lastDropY = py
        if ((array_length dropAges) > (DashboardDemo.maxDrops())) {
            this.retireOldest()
        }
    }

    ; A finger moving across the surface. One ring per step rather than one
    ; ring that follows the pointer: a wake is a row of sources, and a single
    ; source that moves is a source that has no history.
    fn rippleDragTo:void (px:double py:double) {
        if (lastDropX < 0.0) {
            return
        }
        def dx:double (px - lastDropX)
        def dy:double (py - lastDropY)
        ; Every 26 pixels. Closer and the rings pile into one bright blob;
        ; further and the wake reads as a row of separate taps.
        if (((dx * dx) + (dy * dy)) < 676.0) {
            return
        }
        this.ripple(px py)
    }

    fn rippleRelease:void () {
        lastDropX = -1.0
        lastDropY = -1.0
    }

    fn retireOldest:void () {
        def xs:[double]
        def ys:[double]
        def ts:[double]
        def i:int 1
        while (i < (array_length dropAges)) {
            push xs (itemAt dropXs i)
            push ys (itemAt dropYs i)
            push ts (itemAt dropAges i)
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        dropXs = xs
        dropYs = ys
        dropAges = ts
    }

    ; Milliseconds, because that is what a frame loop has.
    ;
    ; NO REBUILD. The ripple is a post-process over the finished picture: not
    ; one box moves, not one word re-wraps, and the only thing that differs
    ; between two frames of it is a number the shader reads. Rebuilding the
    ; tree to advance a clock would make the cheapest effect on the page the
    ; most expensive thing on it — the age is stamped onto the root at paint
    ; time instead, in `displayListJson`.
    fn tick:void (dtMs:double) {
        def dt:double (dtMs / 1000.0)
        def xs:[double]
        def ys:[double]
        def ts:[double]
        def i:int 0
        while (i < (array_length dropAges)) {
            def age:double ((itemAt dropAges i) + dt)
            ; Retired when there is nothing left in it. The threshold is the
            ; renderer's own — a drop whose decay has taken it under a
            ;250th is a drop that is not on the screen.
            if (age < 3.0) {
                push xs (itemAt dropXs i)
                push ys (itemAt dropYs i)
                push ts age
            }
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        dropXs = xs
        dropYs = ys
        dropAges = ts
    }

    ; Is anything still moving? The ripple dies with its own decay, so this
    ; asks the same question the shader does rather than guessing a duration.
    fn busyNow:boolean () {
        return ((array_length dropAges) > 0)
    }

    ; How many touches are in flight. The gate's way of asking whether the
    ; drops really coexist rather than replacing one another.
    fn dropCount:int () {
        return (array_length dropAges)
    }

    fn pageBody:EVGElement () {
        ; THE VIEWPORT IS IN THE STYLESHEET, as `100vh`.
        ;
        ; The page, the sidebar and the hairline stood at a constant 1420 —
        ; the height this page was before it scrolled, kept because it is
        ; taller than the 900 the browser shows and a column taller than the
        ; window looks full. On a window TALLER than 1420 it stops looking
        ; full: an Android tablet in portrait is 2137 page pixels tall, and the
        ; sidebar ended two thirds of the way down with the host's own
        ; background under it.
        ;
        ; `100%` would not have fixed it — a percentage needs a parent with a
        ; definite height and the page's height is the number nobody in the
        ; tree has — so EVG grew `vw`/`vh`, which ask the layout. The layout is
        ; told by `laidOutOnce` below, from the same `pageH` the scroll box
        ; used to be given inline.
        def page (tree Db (Page (props (id "page"))))
        page.addChild((this.sidebar()))
        page.addChild((tree Db (SideEdge)))

        ; THE SCROLL CONTAINER. Its height is the viewport's — `100vh` in the
        ; sheet, like the page and the sidebar — its content is whatever the
        ; current view came to, and the offset is put on before the layout so
        ; the layout can clamp it.
        def scroll (tree Db (Scroll (props (id "db-scroll"))))
        scroll.scrollTop = scrollY

        def content (tree Db (Content (props (id "db-content"))))
        content.addChild((this.viewFor(navPage)))
        content.addChild((tree Db (Note (this.noteFor()))))
        scroll.addChild(content)
        page.addChild(scroll)
        return page
    }

    fn rebuild:void () {
        root = (this.pageBody())
        ; AND THE LAYOUT WITH IT. `pageBody` builds new elements, so the
        ; rectangles in the layout that is cached belong to a tree that no
        ; longer exists — nothing in the new one has been measured at all. A
        ; `laidOut` that handed that back would answer questions about the page
        ; before last: `maxScroll` clamping to a height the current view does
        ; not have, and a hit test finding nothing because every element it
        ; walked is still at zero.
        layoutStale = true
    }

    ; --- layout ---------------------------------------------------------------

    fn laidOut:EVGLayout () {
        if layout {
            if (layoutStale == false) {
                return (unwrap layout)
            }
        }
        return (this.laidOutOnce())
    }

    fn laidOutOnce:EVGLayout () {
        def r:EVGElement (unwrap root)
        def s:EVGStyleSheet (unwrap sheet)
        this.markStates(r)
        ; Between the app saying what IS hovered and the sheet being asked what
        ; that makes true — which is the only ordering an inspector needs a
        ; host to get right. A no-op costing one array length when nothing is
        ; being held, which is every frame nobody is inspecting anything.
        EVGInspect.applyForced(r)
        s.applyTree(r theme)
        def lay (new EVGLayout())
        lay.setPageSize(pageW pageH)
        lay.layout(r)
        ; READ THE OFFSET BACK. The layout clamps `scrollTop` to what there
        ; actually is to scroll, and it is the only thing that knows: the
        ; content's height is its answer. A demo that kept what it ASKED for
        ; would go on believing it was scrolled to 4000 on a page with 300 to
        ; go, and the next scroll-by would do nothing visible for thirteen
        ; presses.
        def sc:EVGElement (this.findEl(r "db-scroll"))
        if ((strlen sc.id) > 0) {
            scrollY = sc.scrollTop
        }
        layout = lay
        layoutStale = false
        return lay
    }

    ; --- scrolling ------------------------------------------------------------

    ; How far there is to go on the current view. Zero before anything has been
    ; laid out, which is the honest answer: nothing has been measured yet.
    fn maxScroll:double () {
        def lay:EVGLayout (this.laidOut())
        def r:EVGElement (unwrap root)
        def sc:EVGElement (this.findEl(r "db-scroll"))
        if ((strlen sc.id) == 0) {
            return 0.0
        }
        return (sc.maxScrollTop())
    }

    fn scrollTo:boolean (y:double) {
        def before:double scrollY
        scrollY = y
        if (scrollY < 0.0) {
            scrollY = 0.0
        }
        this.rebuild()
        ; The rebuild throws the layout away, so the clamp happens on the next
        ; one — ask for it here rather than leaving a number nobody has checked
        ; sitting in the field.
        this.laidOutOnce()
        return (scrollY != before)
    }

    fn scrollBy:boolean (dy:double) {
        return (this.scrollTo((scrollY + dy)))
    }


    fn markStates:void (el:EVGElement) {
        el.isHovered = ((strlen hovered) > 0) && (el.id == hovered)
        el.isFocused = ((strlen focused) > 0) && (el.id == focused)
        el.isPressed = ((strlen pressed) > 0) && (el.id == pressed)
        def i:int 0
        while (i < (array_length el.children)) {
            this.markStates((itemAt el.children i))
            i = (i + 1)
        }
    }

    fn findEl:EVGElement (el:EVGElement id:string) {
        if (el.id == id) {
            return el
        }
        def i:int 0
        while (i < (array_length el.children)) {
            def found:EVGElement (this.findEl((itemAt el.children i) id))
            if ((strlen found.id) > 0) {
                return found
            }
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        return (EVGElement.createDiv())
    }

    ; --- the ink that is on no element -----------------------------------------
    ;
    ; What `.db-ink-line` resolves to under the theme that is showing.
    ;
    ; The chart is Vela's and the scroll indicator is two rectangles, so
    ; neither is an element the sheet can write to — and both had their colours
    ; typed into this file, which meant a theme could repaint the frame around
    ; the biggest picture on the page and not the picture. So the colours stay
    ; in the sheet on classes nothing wears, and this asks the sheet the
    ; question an element would have asked: a bare element carrying the class,
    ; styled, and read back.
    ;
    ; Nothing is added to the tree and nothing is measured — `applyTo` writes
    ; the properties the rules declare, and `color` is the only one these rules
    ; declare.
    fn ink:EVGColor (cls:string) {
        def s:EVGStyleSheet (unwrap sheet)
        def probe:EVGElement (EVGElement.createDiv())
        probe.className = cls
        s.applyTo(probe theme)
        if probe.color {
            def got:EVGColor (unwrap probe.color)
            ; `isSet`, not "is the field there": an element that was never
            ; given a colour carries `EVGColor.noColor()`, so the field is
            ; always there and the fallback below was unreachable. An unset
            ; colour writes "none" into the Vega specification, which is a
            ; transparent chart rather than a visible mistake.
            if got.isSet {
                return got
            }
        }
        ; No `color` in the sheet for that class — a name that is not in the
        ; file, in practice. Black, which is what an element with no colour
        ; draws as, so a mistake looks like one instead of like a theme.
        return (EVGColor.parse("#000000"))
    }

    ; The same answer as a string, because a Vega specification is JSON and
    ; takes its colours as text.
    fn inkHex:string (cls:string) {
        def c:EVGColor (this.ink(cls))
        return (c.toHexString())
    }

    ; --- input ----------------------------------------------------------------

    fn ring:[string] () {
        def a:[string]
        def i:int 0
        while (i < (array_length range.items)) {
            def it:UiItem (itemAt range.items i)
            push a ("db-range-" + it.value)
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        return a
    }

    fn selectRange:boolean (value:string) {
        if (range.value == value) {
            return false
        }
        range.value = value
        focused = ("db-range-" + value)
        this.rebuild()
        return true
    }

    ; --- the table's interaction ----------------------------------------------

    ; THE WRITE-BACK. `SortableCtl` has arrived at an order; `TableCtl.records`
    ; is the one that gets drawn. This is the only place the two meet, and it
    ; runs after a drop rather than during a drag, because a half-finished
    ; gesture is not an order anybody asked for.
    ; THE WRITE-BACK, and it moves ONE record. `SortableCtl` has arrived at an
    ; order for the rows on screen; `table.records` is the list that gets
    ; drawn. Splicing the dragged record to where the sortable put it is O(n)
    ; and rebuilding the whole list from the sortable was O(n²) — see
    ; `syncOrder`.
    ;
    ; It runs after a drop rather than during a drag, because a half-finished
    ; gesture is not an order anybody asked for.
    fn applyOrder:void (moved:string) {
        if ((strlen moved) == 0) {
            return
        }
        def from:int (table.recordAt(moved))
        if (from < 0) {
            return
        }
        ; Where the sortable put it among the rows on screen, and which record
        ; it now sits before. That record's position in the FULL list is the
        ; destination.
        def at:int (order.indexOf(moved))
        if (at < 0) {
            return
        }
        def beforeKey:string ""
        if ((at + 1) < (array_length order.items)) {
            def nextIt:UiItem (itemAt order.items (at + 1))
            beforeKey = nextIt.value
        }

        def rec:UiRecord (itemAt table.records from)
        def out:[UiRecord]
        def i:int 0
        def placed:boolean false
        while (i < (array_length table.records)) {
            def r:UiRecord (itemAt table.records i)
            if (r.key != moved) {
                if (r.key == beforeKey) {
                    push out rec
                    placed = true
                }
                push out r
            }
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        if (placed == false) {
            ; Dropped at the end of the visible window, so it goes after
            ; everything that was in front of it.
            push out rec
        }
        ; Only when nothing was lost. A shorter list would mean a row vanished,
        ; and drawing that is worse than ignoring the drag.
        if ((array_length out) == (array_length table.records)) {
            table.records = out
            ; A hand-made order is not a sorted one, so the header stops
            ; claiming it is. The alternative is a column that says
            ; "ascending" over rows in an order it did not choose.
            table.sortKey = ""
        }
    }

    fn tabValueOf:string (target:string) {
        def i:int 0
        while (i < (array_length tabs.items)) {
            def it:UiItem (itemAt tabs.items i)
            if (target == ("db-tab-" + it.value)) {
                return it.value
            }
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        return ""
    }

    ; A sidebar link. Nothing NAVIGATES — there is one page — but the current
    ; link moves, which is the visible and the announced half of navigation
    ; both, and it is the half a layout gets wrong.
    fn navPress:boolean (target:string) {
        if ((strlen target) < 8) {
            return false
        }
        if (((substring target 0 7)) != "db-nav-") {
            return false
        }
        navPage = (substring target 7 (strlen target))
        focused = target
        ; BACK TO THE TOP. Following a link and landing halfway down the next
        ; page is what a browser does not do, and the reason it does not is
        ; that the offset belonged to the page you left.
        scrollY = 0.0
        this.rebuild()
        return true
    }

    fn press:boolean (target:string) {
        if (this.navPress(target)) {
            return true
        }
        def i:int 0
        while (i < (array_length range.items)) {
            def it:UiItem (itemAt range.items i)
            if (target == ("db-range-" + it.value)) {
                focused = target
                return (this.selectRange(it.value))
            }
            i = (i + 1)
        }

        def tabValue:string (this.tabValueOf(target))
        if ((strlen tabValue) > 0) {
            focused = target
            if (tabs.value == tabValue) {
                return false
            }
            tabs.value = tabValue
            this.rebuild()
            return true
        }

        ; The table's own targets: the select-all box, a row's checkbox, a
        ; sortable column header, and the pager. `TableCtl` decides what each
        ; one does; this only says where focus went.
        if (table.pressTid(target)) {
            focused = target
            this.rebuild()
            return true
        }

        ; A press on a grip picks the row up; a press on it again drops it.
        def dragValue:string (order.valueOf(target))
        if ((strlen dragValue) > 0) {
            focused = target
            if (order.activeValue == dragValue) {
                order.drop()
                this.applyOrder(dragValue)
            } {
                order.pickUp(dragValue)
            }
            this.rebuild()
            return true
        }
        return false
    }

    ; A key that reaches a grip is a reorder; anything else goes to the table.
    fn tableKey:boolean (k:string) {
        def dragValue:string (order.valueOf(focused))
        if ((strlen dragValue) > 0) {
            def before:string order.activeValue
            def moved:string (order.keyDown(focused k))
            ; A DROP is the moment the order becomes real. `activeValue` going
            ; empty after being set is what a drop looks like from here, and
            ; a cancel looks the same — which is why `applyOrder` is written to
            ; be safe to run either way: after a cancel the sortable has put
            ; the row back and the write-back is a no-op.
            if ((strlen before) > 0) {
                if ((strlen order.activeValue) == 0) {
                    this.applyOrder(before)
                }
            }
            if ((strlen moved) > 0) {
                focused = moved
            }
            this.rebuild()
            return true
        }
        if (table.key(focused k)) {
            this.rebuild()
            return true
        }
        return false
    }

    ; Arrows move AND select, which is what a radio group does — a reader
    ; arrowing through one hears each option chosen as they land on it.
    fn key:boolean (k:string) {
        ; The range buttons are the only arrow-driven thing on the page that
        ; is NOT in the table, so they get first refusal and everything else
        ; falls through to the grid.
        def r:[string] (this.ring())
        def n:int (array_length r)
        def at:int (0 - 1)
        def i:int 0
        while (i < n) {
            if ((itemAt r i) == focused) {
                at = i
            }
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        if (at < 0) {
            if (this.tableKey(k)) {
                return true
            }
            ; Nothing else wanted it, so it goes to the scroll region. LAST,
            ; not first: a Home that moves between range buttons and a Home
            ; that jumps to the top of the page are the same key, and the
            ; control that has focus is the one that gets to say which.
            return (this.scrollKey(k))
        }
        def next:int at
        if ((k == "ArrowRight") || (k == "ArrowDown")) {
            next = (at + 1)
        }
        if ((k == "ArrowLeft") || (k == "ArrowUp")) {
            next = (at - 1)
        }
        if (k == "Home") {
            next = 0
        }
        if (k == "End") {
            next = (n - 1)
        }
        if (next == at) {
            return false
        }
        if (next < 0) {
            next = (n - 1)
        }
        if (next >= n) {
            next = 0
        }
        def it:UiItem (itemAt range.items next)
        focused = (itemAt r next)
        return (this.selectRange(it.value))
    }

    ; What the keys do to the main region. A page is the viewport less a
    ; couple of lines of overlap, the way every document reader does it: land
    ; with something you have already read still on the screen.
    fn scrollKey:boolean (k:string) {
        def page:double (pageH - 40.0)
        if (k == "PageDown") { return (this.scrollBy(page)) }
        if (k == "PageUp")   { return (this.scrollBy((0.0 - page))) }
        if (k == "Home")     { return (this.scrollTo(0.0)) }
        if (k == "End")      { return (this.scrollTo((this.maxScroll()))) }
        if (k == "ArrowDown") { return (this.scrollBy(48.0)) }
        if (k == "ArrowUp")   { return (this.scrollBy(-48.0)) }
        return false
    }

    fn setHover:void (id:string) { hovered = id }
    fn setPressed:void (id:string) { pressed = id }
    fn setFocus:void (id:string) { focused = id }

    ; --- the theme ------------------------------------------------------------

    fn themeName:string () { return theme }

    ; The tree is NOT rebuilt: not one element gains or loses a class, and the
    ; elements themselves say nothing about colour. What changes is which rules
    ; the sheet matches on the next pass, so the only thing to invalidate is
    ; the answer that pass produced.
    fn setTheme:boolean (name:string) {
        if (name == theme) {
            return false
        }
        theme = name
        layoutStale = true
        return true
    }

    ; --- the picture ----------------------------------------------------------

    ; The page, and then the chart on top of it at the box the layout reserved.
    ;
    ; The chart is NOT in the element tree — see the header. This is where the
    ; two halves meet, and the only place either of them knows about the other.
    ;
    ; The list is returned as an OBJECT, and `displayListJson` below is one line
    ; over it. A host in the same process wants the list: the Kotlin/Android
    ; host (`gallery/ui/android`) reads `cmds` straight out of this, so nothing
    ; is serialised and nothing is parsed back. The browser gets the JSON
    ; because a page has to parse something anyway.
    fn display:EVGDisplayList () {
        def lay:EVGLayout (this.laidOutOnce())
        def r:EVGElement (unwrap root)
        ; The ripple's state, stamped on at paint time. It is not part of the
        ; tree's shape — nothing about the page depends on it — so it does not
        ; belong in `pageBody` where it would make every frame of the effect a
        ; rebuild.
        r.rippleXs = dropXs
        r.rippleYs = dropYs
        r.rippleAges = dropAges
        def dl (new EVGDisplayList())
        dl.build(r)
        chartFrom = 0
        chartTo = 0

        def scroll:EVGElement (this.findEl(r "db-scroll"))
        def box:EVGElement (this.findEl(r "db-chart-box"))
        if ((strlen box.id) > 0) {
            def parser (new VlJsonParser())
            def specJson:VlJson (parser.parse((this.specText())))
            if parser.ok {
                def rt (new VlRuntime())
                def scene:VlSceneMark (rt.run(specJson))
                if ((array_length rt.errors) == 0) {
                    def list:VlCommandList (VlSceneCommands.build(scene))
                    def tr (new UITextRenderer())
                    def bridge (new VlEvgList())
                    ; CLIPPED BY HAND, and it has to be. The chart is appended
                    ; after `dl.build(root)` has walked the whole tree — that
                    ; is what puts it outside the element tree — so it is also
                    ; outside every clip the walk pushed and popped. Scrolled
                    ; up, an unclipped chart draws straight across the sidebar.
                    ;
                    ; The box it is clipped to is the scroll region, which is
                    ; the same box the walk would have clipped it to had it
                    ; been a child.
                    chartFrom = (array_length dl.cmds)
                    dl.addClip(scroll.calculatedX scroll.calculatedY scroll.calculatedWidth scroll.calculatedHeight)
                    ; The axis labels sit to the left of and below the plot,
                    ; and Vela measures its own origin — so the offset is the
                    ; box's corner and nothing else. `calculatedY` has already
                    ; had the scroll offset applied to it by the layout, so the
                    ; chart travels with the card it belongs to for free.
                    bridge.emit(dl list tr (box.calculatedX + gutterX) box.calculatedY 1.0)
                    dl.addClipEnd()
                    chartTo = (array_length dl.cmds)
                }
            }
        }

        ; --- THE SCROLLBAR ----------------------------------------------------
        ;
        ; Drawn, not built. It is the one thing on the page whose size and
        ; position are not known until AFTER the layout has run — the thumb is
        ; a fraction of the content height — and an element that has to be
        ; measured before it can be placed would need a second layout pass to
        ; put in the tree.
        ;
        ; So it is two rectangles in the display list, and it is honest about
        ; what that costs: it is an INDICATOR, not a control. There is nothing
        ; to grab, because there is no element to hit. The wheel, the keys and
        ; the sidebar do the scrolling.
        if ((strlen scroll.id) > 0) {
            def travel:double (scroll.maxScrollTop())
            if (travel > 0.0) {
                def trackX:double (((scroll.calculatedX + scroll.calculatedWidth) - 10.0))
                def trackY:double (scroll.calculatedY + 6.0)
                def trackH:double (scroll.calculatedHeight - 12.0)
                dl.addRect(trackX trackY 4.0 trackH (this.ink("db-ink-track")))
                def visible:double (scroll.clientHeight())
                def frac:double (visible / scroll.scrollHeight)
                def thumbH:double (trackH * frac)
                if (thumbH < 24.0) {
                    thumbH = 24.0
                }
                def at:double (scrollY / travel)
                def thumbY:double (trackY + (at * (trackH - thumbH)))
                dl.addRect(trackX thumbY 4.0 thumbH (this.ink("db-ink-thumb")))
            }
        }
        return dl
    }

    fn displayListJson:string () {
        def dl:EVGDisplayList (this.display())
        return (dl.toJson())
    }

    ; How many commands the chart contributed. The gate's way of asking whether
    ; there IS a chart, without asserting a particular outline.
    fn chartCommandCount:int () {
        def parser (new VlJsonParser())
        def specJson:VlJson (parser.parse((this.specText())))
        if (! parser.ok) {
            return 0
        }
        def rt (new VlRuntime())
        def scene:VlSceneMark (rt.run(specJson))
        if ((array_length rt.errors) > 0) {
            return 0
        }
        def list:VlCommandList (VlSceneCommands.build(scene))
        def dl (new EVGDisplayList())
        def tr (new UITextRenderer())
        def bridge (new VlEvgList())
        bridge.emit(dl list tr 0.0 0.0 1.0)
        return (array_length dl.cmds)
    }

    fn specErrors:string () {
        def parser (new VlJsonParser())
        def specJson:VlJson (parser.parse((this.specText())))
        if (! parser.ok) {
            return ("json: " + parser.err)
        }
        def rt (new VlRuntime())
        def scene:VlSceneMark (rt.run(specJson))
        if ((array_length rt.errors) > 0) {
            return (itemAt rt.errors 0)
        }
        return ""
    }

    fn hitId:string (px:double py:double) {
        this.displayListJson()
        def r:EVGElement (unwrap root)
        def hit (new EVGHitTest())
        return (hit.idAt(r px py))
    }

    ; The same hit test against the layout that is ALREADY there.
    ;
    ; `hitId` draws the whole picture first — the Vela chart included — because
    ; a browser calls it once per click and a guaranteed-fresh layout is worth
    ; more than the microseconds. A host that paints every frame has already
    ; laid the page out, and running the chart's runtime again for every touch
    ; event is tens of milliseconds spent on geometry nothing will look at: the
    ; chart is not in the element tree, so it can never be what was hit.
    ;
    ; `laidOut` returns the cached layout when there is one and lays out once
    ; when a rebuild threw it away, so this is never a hit test against stale
    ; rectangles.
    fn hitIdCached:string (px:double py:double) {
        def r:EVGElement (unwrap root)
        this.laidOut()
        def hit (new EVGHitTest())
        return (hit.idAt(r px py))
    }

    fn a11yJson:string (generation:int focusId:string) {
        this.displayListJson()
        def r:EVGElement (unwrap root)
        def b (new EVGA11yFromTree())
        def t:EVGA11yTree (b.build(r "Dashboard demo" generation focusId))
        return (t.toJson())
    }

    ; --- the inspector --------------------------------------------------------
    ;
    ; The fourth channel, beside displayListJson / hitId / a11yJson: what the
    ; tree IS. `EVGInspect` walks the same laid-out tree the other three walk,
    ; so nothing here is a second opinion about geometry.

    fn inspectJson:string (gen:int) {
        this.displayListJson()
        def r:EVGElement (unwrap root)
        def w:double (this.widthPx())
        def h:double (this.heightPx())
        return (EVGInspect.treeOf(r "Dashboard demo" gen w h))
    }

    fn inspectNodeJson:string (path:string) {
        def r:EVGElement (unwrap root)
        this.laidOut()
        def sh:EVGStyleSheet (unwrap sheet)
        return (EVGInspect.nodeOfIn(r path sh))
    }

    ; --- the sheet as an editable input ---------------------------------------
    ;
    ; The element tree is this app's OUTPUT and the stylesheet is its INPUT, so
    ; the sheet is the thing an inspector can hand back changed. Nothing is
    ; intercepted and no value is held over the app's head: it re-parses and
    ; re-cascades exactly as it did at `init`, and everything downstream — the
    ; layout, the display list, the hit test — follows because it always did.
    ;
    ; `reload` and not a fresh `EVGStyleSheet`: a new sheet would restart the
    ; generation counter and `applyTo` would skip every element still holding
    ; the old one. `EVGStyleSheet.reload` says the rest.
    fn inspectCss:string () {
        return cssText
    }

    fn inspectSetCss:void (css:string) {
        cssText = css
        def sh:EVGStyleSheet (unwrap sheet)
        sh.reload(css)
        ; The cascade will rewrite every element — `reload` bumped the
        ; generation, so nothing in the tree is holding what the sheet says any
        ; more — but the LAYOUT is cached separately and does not know that.
        layoutStale = true
        this.displayListJson()
    }

    ; --- states held on -------------------------------------------------------
    ;
    ; Chrome's `:hov` toggles. The table lives in `EVGInspectForce` and is
    ; keyed by node path, so it outlives the rebuild this page does on every
    ; input; `laidOutOnce` applies it between marking this app's own states and
    ; running the cascade.
    fn inspectForce:void (path:string bits:int) {
        EVGInspect.forceState(path bits)
        layoutStale = true
        this.displayListJson()
    }

    fn inspectClearForced:void () {
        EVGInspect.clearForced()
        layoutStale = true
        this.displayListJson()
    }

    fn inspectStyleErrors:string () {
        def sh:EVGStyleSheet (unwrap sheet)
        def out:string "["
        def i:int 0
        while (i < (array_length sh.errors)) {
            if (i > 0) {
                out = (out + ",")
            }
            out = (out + (EVGInspect.jsonString((itemAt sh.errors i))))
            i = (i + 1)
        }
        return (out + "]")
    }

    ; `laidOut` and not `displayListJson`: a hit test wants the layout that is
    ; already there, and re-rendering the page — the Vela chart included — for
    ; every pointer move is tens of milliseconds spent on geometry nothing will
    ; look at. Same reason `hitIdCached` exists.
    fn inspectHitPath:string (px:double py:double) {
        def r:EVGElement (unwrap root)
        this.laidOut()
        return (EVGInspect.hitOf(r px py))
    }

    ; The attributed frame. It is the element tree's commands only — the chart
    ; is appended after the walk and belongs to no element, so it has nothing
    ; to be attributed to and is left out rather than blamed on the box it
    ; happens to sit in.
    fn inspectFrameJson:string () {
        def r:EVGElement (unwrap root)
        this.laidOut()
        return (EVGInspect.frameOf(r))
    }

    fn a11yProblems:[string] () {
        this.displayListJson()
        def r:EVGElement (unwrap root)
        def b (new EVGA11yFromTree())
        def t:EVGA11yTree (b.build(r "Dashboard demo" 1 ""))
        return (t.lint())
    }

    fn styleErrorCount:int () {
        def s:EVGStyleSheet (unwrap sheet)
        return (array_length s.errors)
    }

    fn styleErrorAt:string (i:int) {
        def s:EVGStyleSheet (unwrap sheet)
        return (itemAt s.errors i)
    }

    fn widthPx:double () { return pageW }
    fn heightPx:double () { return pageH }
}
